{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_019stVWRvhBmheJXZN88CA1H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q3: What makes a system truly agentic? What does NOT qualify?**\n\n---\n\n**Core of the answer:**\n\nThe defining criterion is **who controls the control flow** — the LLM or the developer.\n\n**Three necessary conditions for agency:**\n1. **Dynamic action selection** — LLM chooses which tool/action at each step\n2. **Observation-driven iteration** — tool outputs feed back as context; model reasons about next move\n3. **LLM-controlled termination** — model decides when done, not a hard-coded loop\n\n**What does NOT qualify (common traps):**\n| Pattern | Why it's not agentic |\n|---------|----------------------|\n| Fixed prompt chain (Sequential LangChain) | Developer pre-wires the sequence |\n| RAG pipeline (retrieve → generate) | Steps are fixed; model doesn't choose whether to retrieve |\n| Intent router | LLM classifies once; dispatch logic is in code |\n| Map-reduce over documents | Static fanout |\n\n**What does qualify:** ReAct loops, OpenAI function-calling with iteration, LangGraph with conditional edges, multi-agent systems like Autogen/CrewAI.\n\n**Key tradeoff:** Agency = flexibility, but costs predictability, debuggability, and money. For well-defined repeatable tasks, a chain is almost always better.\n\nNext up is **Q4: When is agentic architecture the wrong solution?**"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":360,"cache_read_input_tokens":62494,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":360,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee783806-4a9a-4547-8bf5-020c8c64d4d9","uuid":"bf45d9e2-bbb9-4b24-8789-78c7cd47c2a5"}
